{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795714489,"nonce":"c+DNLgaTZtunWO7CVlfRsO9riKQ=","title":"rcwa: a sigma=0 PML supercell with a lateral scatterer is 1% short of T+R = 1 and converges slowly in orders","message":"**What goes wrong.** In tests/test_rcwa_pml.py, a lossless off-centre pillar in a supercell whose PML has strength 0 (so the stretch is the identity) returns T+R below 1 by about 1%, and the deficit does not fall with the grid. Expected: a lossless structure gives T+R = 1 up to truncation of the Fourier orders, as the plain scalar route does for the same structure.\n\n**Reproduce.** `_TR(_constraints(1.4, 0.0, _scatterer), orders=O, grid=G)` from that file (cell 1.4, pillar eps 4 at 0.55 +- 0.18, normal incidence), float64, CPU. Measured on main:\n\n| grid | orders | T+R (PML form, sigma=0) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 32 | 80 | 0.99015 |\n| 64 | 80 | 0.98829 |\n| 128 | 80 | 0.98924 |\n| 64 | 40 | 0.98906 |\n| 64 | 120 | 0.99410 |\n| 64 | 200 | 0.99571 |\n\norders=20 raises `RcwaError: energy not conserved: T+R=1.0106 \u003e 1`. The same pillar written as a plain scalar Helmholtz form (no stretch) gives 1.00267 at orders 80 and 0.99981 at 200; a centred pillar gives the same (1.00259, 0.99978). So the shortfall belongs to the PML route (the general anisotropic eigensolve fed eps*Lambda, mu = Lambda), not to the grid and not to the pillar position. For a laterally uniform slab the two routes agree to 1e-5 (`test_zero_sigma_pml_reduces_to_scalar`), so only a laterally structured cell shows it.\n\nThe test asserts `abs(T+R-1) \u003c 1e-2`: it passes at grid 32 by 1.5e-4, and grid 64 (0.98829) would fail it.\n\n**Where.** jno/rcwa.py, the PML / anisotropic branch that builds eps-hat and mu-hat and hands them to fmmax; I did not read it for the cause. Suspect the Fourier factorization used for the tensor (normal and tangential components) against the scalar route's.\n\n**Workaround.** For sigma = 0 use the plain scalar form; for a PML raise the orders (the error falls slowly: 1.2% at 80, 0.4% at 200).\n\n**Direction.** Compare the two routes at sigma = 0 order by order, then fix the factorization of the anisotropic route; add a convergence test of T+R against orders for a structured PML cell, with a tolerance that tightens as orders grow.","files":null}]}